---
import StandardPageLayout from "../../layouts/StandardPageLayout.astro";
import PageToc from "../../components/PageToc.astro";
import "../../styles/standards-poster.css";
import "../../styles/extended.css";
import HistoryTimeline from "../../components/HistoryTimeline.astro";
import history from "../../data/history.json";
import metadata from "../../data/standards/ias/ias29.json";

---

<StandardPageLayout {...metadata}>
  <Fragment slot="toc">
    <PageToc
      items={[
        {
          id: "s1",
          label: "Conceptual Framework",
        },
        {
          id: "s2",
          label: "Scope & Definitions",
        },
        {
          id: "s3",
          label: "Identifying Hyperinflation",
        },
        {
          id: "s4",
          label: "Restating the SOFP",
        },
        {
          id: "s5",
          label: "Restating the SOPL",
        },
        {
          id: "s6",
          label: "Monetary vs Non-Monetary Restatement",
        },
        {
          id: "s7",
          label: "Gain or Loss on Net Monetary Position",
        },
        {
          id: "s8",
          label: "Restating Comparatives & Consolidation",
        },
        {
          id: "s9",
          label: "Disclosure",
        },
        {
          id: "s10",
          label: "Judgments & Estimates",
        },
        {
          id: "s11",
          label: "IFRS vs US GAAP",
        },
        {
          id: "s12",
          label: "Statement Mapping",
        },
        {
          id: "s13",
          label: "Related Standards",
        },
        {
          id: "s14",
          label: "Glossary",
        },
        {
          id: "history",
          label: "History & Development",
        },
      ]}
    />
  </Fragment>

  <div class="wrap">
    <!-- ============ SECTION 1 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Conceptual Framework Linkage</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci">
        <div class="ctitle ci">Why historical cost becomes meaningless under hyperinflation</div>
        <div class="principle">Historical cost financial statements assume the measuring unit (currency) is reasonably stable. In a HYPERINFLATIONARY economy, this assumption breaks down completely — reporting in units of currency without adjustment is misleading, because even the RESULTS OF OPERATIONS AND FINANCIAL POSITION expressed in the local currency without restatement are not meaningful. Comparing amounts from different time periods (even within the same year) is like comparing apples to oranges.</div>
        <div class="elabel">HOW IAS 29 MAPS TO THE FRAMEWORK</div>
        <div class="check-list">
          <span><strong style="font-weight:600">Faithful representation</strong> — restating financial statements in terms of the measuring unit CURRENT at the reporting date restores meaningful comparability that historical cost figures would otherwise destroy</span>
          <span><strong style="font-weight:600">Comparability</strong> — without restatement, prior period comparatives are effectively expressed in a fundamentally different "unit of measure," making period-on-period comparison meaningless</span>
          <span><strong style="font-weight:600">Relevance</strong> — a gain or loss on the entity's net monetary position becomes a genuinely material, decision-useful figure only once restatement makes it visible</span>
        </div>
        <div class="note">IAS 29 is NOT a system of current cost accounting — it is a restatement mechanism using a GENERAL PRICE INDEX to express ALL amounts in the measuring unit current at the reporting date, applied on top of whichever measurement basis (cost or fair value) the entity already uses under other Standards.</div>
      </div>

      <div class="card cs">
        <div class="ctitle cs">Measurement principles applied by IAS 29</div>
        <div class="principle">IAS 29 restates the ENTIRE financial statements by reference to changes in a general price index — it changes the UNIT OF MEASURE, not the underlying recognition or measurement basis of individual items.</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.3fr 1fr;"><span>Item type</span><span>Restatement approach</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.3fr 1fr;"><span>Monetary items</span><span>NOT restated — already expressed in the current measuring unit at year end</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.3fr 1fr;"><span>Non-monetary items at historical cost</span><span>Restated by applying the change in the general price index from the date of acquisition to the reporting date</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.3fr 1fr;"><span>All SOPL items</span><span>Restated by applying the change in the general price index from the dates the items were originally recorded</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="callout info" style="margin-bottom:0;"> <strong style="font-weight:600">Qualitative characteristics in action:</strong> restating in a general price index preserves <strong style="font-weight:600">faithful representation</strong> without abandoning historical cost's underlying recognition principles — it is purely a UNIT-OF-MEASURE correction.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 2 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Scope & Applicability</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw">
        <div class="ctitle cw">What IAS 29 covers</div>
        <div class="principle">IAS 29 shall be applied to the financial statements, including the CONSOLIDATED financial statements, of any entity whose FUNCTIONAL CURRENCY is the currency of a hyperinflationary economy.</div>
        <div class="elabel">MANDATORY, NOT OPTIONAL</div>
        <div style="font-size:13px;color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.6;">Once an economy is identified as hyperinflationary, restatement is REQUIRED for all entities with that functional currency — there is no policy choice. Restated financial statements REPLACE the primary (unadjusted) financial statements; they are NOT supplementary information presented alongside the unadjusted figures.</div>
        <div class="note">IAS 29 does NOT establish an absolute rate (e.g. a specific percentage) that triggers hyperinflation — it provides characteristics (Section 3) that indicate its existence, requiring judgement.</div>
      </div>

      <div class="card cd">
        <div class="ctitle cd">Key definitions you must know</div>
        <div class="glossary-item">
          <div class="glossary-term">General price index</div>
          <div class="glossary-def">An index reflecting changes in general purchasing power, used to restate financial statement amounts to the measuring unit current at the reporting date.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="glossary-item">
          <div class="glossary-term">Monetary items</div>
          <div class="glossary-def">Money held, and items to be received or paid, in fixed or determinable amounts of money — the same definition used in IAS 21.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;">
          <div class="glossary-term">Gain or loss on net monetary position</div>
          <div class="glossary-def">The gain or loss arising from holding a net monetary asset or liability position during a period of inflation — a NEW category of item that appears only under restated hyperinflationary reporting.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 3 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>Identifying Hyperinflation — The Indicators</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2">
        <div class="ctitle ci">Characteristics of the economic environment indicating hyperinflation</div>
        <div class="check-list">
          <span>The general population prefers to keep its wealth in non-monetary assets or in a relatively stable foreign currency; amounts of LOCAL currency held are immediately invested to maintain purchasing power</span>
          <span>The general population regards monetary amounts NOT in terms of the local currency but in terms of a relatively stable foreign currency; prices may be quoted in that currency</span>
          <span>Sales and purchases on credit take place at prices that compensate for the expected loss of purchasing power during the credit period, EVEN IF the period is short</span>
          <span>Interest rates, wages, and prices are linked to a price index</span>
          <span>The CUMULATIVE inflation rate over 3 years approaches, or exceeds, 100%</span>
        </div>
        <div class="callout warn" style="margin-bottom:0;"> <strong style="font-weight:600">The 100% cumulative 3-year figure is a GUIDELINE, not a rigid bright-line test</strong> — IAS 29 explicitly states it is a matter of JUDGEMENT when restatement becomes necessary, based on the qualitative characteristics collectively, not a single quantitative trigger alone.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 4 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>Restating the Statement of Financial Position (with worked example)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs">
        <div class="ctitle cs">The restatement formula</div>
        <div class="formula-box">
          <div class="f-eq">Restated amount = Historical amount × (General price index at reporting date ÷ General price index at the date the item was acquired/incurred)</div>
        </div>
        <div style="font-size:13px;color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.6;">Applied to ALL non-monetary items carried at historical cost (or historical cost less depreciation) — items already stated at current amounts (e.g. NRV, fair value) at the reporting date are NOT restated using the index, since they already reflect current values.</div>
      </div>

      <div class="card cw">
        <div class="ctitle cw">Worked example — restating an item of PPE</div>
        <div class="worked">
          <div class="wk-title">Example 1: Restating a machine's historical cost</div>
          <div class="wk-body">
            A machine was purchased for 400,000 local currency units (LCU) when the general price index was 100. At the current reporting date, the general price index is 340. Accumulated depreciation to date (unrestated) is 120,000 LCU (30% of cost).
          </div>
          <div class="wk-answer">
            <strong>Restated cost</strong> = 400,000 × (340 ÷ 100) = <strong style="color:var(--color-text-info);">1,360,000 LCU</strong><br>
            <strong>Restated accumulated depreciation</strong> = 1,360,000 × 30% = <strong style="color:var(--color-text-info);">408,000 LCU</strong><br>
            <strong>Restated carrying amount</strong> = 1,360,000 − 408,000 = <strong style="color:var(--color-text-success);">952,000 LCU</strong>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="note">The SAME index ratio applied to cost is also applied to accumulated depreciation, preserving the original depreciation percentage relationship in restated terms.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 5 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>Restating the Statement of Profit or Loss</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2">
        <div class="ctitle cd">Every SOPL item restated from its own transaction date — with worked example</div>
        <div class="principle">ALL items in the statement of profit or loss are restated by applying the change in the general price index from the DATES the items of income and expenses were ORIGINALLY recorded — this is far more granular than a single average-rate approximation, since transactions genuinely occurred at different points across the period, each with a different price level.</div>
        <div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;">
          <div class="wk-title">Example 2: Restating a single sale transaction</div>
          <div class="wk-body">
            A sale of 50,000 LCU was recorded on a date when the general price index was 280. At the reporting date, the index is 340.
          </div>
          <div class="wk-answer">
            <strong>Restated sale</strong> = 50,000 × (340 ÷ 280) = <strong style="color:var(--color-text-info);">60,714 LCU</strong> (rounded)
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 6 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>Monetary vs Non-Monetary Restatement</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci">
        <div class="ctitle ci">Why monetary items are NOT restated</div>
        <div style="font-size:13px;color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.6;">Monetary items are NOT restated because they are ALREADY expressed in the monetary unit current at the reporting date. Cash, receivables, and payables retain their nominal value — but this is precisely why holding NET monetary assets during inflation causes a LOSS of purchasing power (and holding a net monetary LIABILITY position causes a GAIN), captured separately (Section 7).</div>
      </div>

      <div class="card cw">
        <div class="ctitle cw">Classification recap</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;"><span>Monetary (NOT restated)</span><span>Non-monetary (RESTATED)</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;"><span>Cash, receivables, payables</span><span>PPE, inventory, intangible assets (at cost)</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;"><span>Loans and borrowings</span><span>Share capital, retained earnings, other equity items</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="note">Equity items are treated as NON-monetary and ARE restated from the date they were contributed/arose — this is a distinguishing feature versus the ordinary IAS 21 monetary/non-monetary split, since equity is restated here but plays no comparable "translation" role under IAS 21.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 7 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>Gain or Loss on Net Monetary Position (with worked example)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs span2">
        <div class="ctitle cs">Recognising the purchasing-power effect of holding monetary items</div>
        <div class="principle">The gain or loss on the net monetary position is included in profit or loss, and separately disclosed. It arises because holding monetary assets during inflation causes a LOSS of purchasing power, while holding monetary LIABILITIES causes a GAIN of purchasing power (since the amount ultimately repaid is worth less in real terms).</div>
        <div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;">
          <div class="wk-title">Example 3: Net monetary liability position generates a gain</div>
          <div class="wk-body">
            An entity holds net monetary LIABILITIES (borrowings exceeding cash and receivables) of 2,000,000 LCU throughout the reporting period, during which the general price index rises from 300 to 340 (a 13.3% increase).
          </div>
          <div class="wk-answer">
            Since the entity owes a FIXED amount of currency that is worth LESS in real terms by year end, holding a net monetary LIABILITY position generates a GAIN on net monetary position, approximately:<br>
            2,000,000 × (340 − 300) ÷ 300 ≈ <strong style="color:var(--color-text-success);">266,667 LCU gain</strong> (simplified; in practice calculated via detailed restatement of the monetary position's movements through the period)
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="callout warn" style="margin-bottom:0;"> Conversely, an entity holding significant CASH or receivables (a net monetary ASSET position) during hyperinflation suffers a LOSS on its net monetary position — a frequently tested distinction, and one reason entities in hyperinflationary economies often prefer holding non-monetary assets or foreign currency, consistent with the population behaviour described in Section 3.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 8 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>Restating Comparatives & Consolidation</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd">
        <div class="ctitle cd">Corresponding figures for the previous period</div>
        <div style="font-size:13px;color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.6;">Corresponding figures for the PREVIOUS reporting period, whether based on a historical cost approach or a current cost approach, are restated by applying a general price index so that the comparative financial statements are presented in terms of the measuring unit current at the END of the LATEST reporting period.</div>
      </div>

      <div class="card cn">
        <div class="ctitle cn">Order of operations in consolidation</div>
        <div style="font-size:13px;color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.6;">Where a foreign operation's functional currency is that of a hyperinflationary economy, its financial statements are FIRST restated under IAS 29, and ONLY THEN translated into the presentation currency under IAS 21's rules — the two standards apply SEQUENTIALLY, not simultaneously, with IAS 29 always coming FIRST.</div>
        <div class="note">Once an economy ceases to be hyperinflationary, the entity discontinues preparing statements under IAS 29 and USES, as the historical cost basis for subsequent financial statements, the amounts expressed in the measuring unit current at the END of the PREVIOUS (last hyperinflationary) reporting period.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 9 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>Disclosure Requirements</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2">
        <div class="ctitle cd">Required disclosures</div>
        <div class="check-list">
          <span>The fact that the financial statements and corresponding figures for previous periods have been restated for changes in the general purchasing power of the functional currency, and, as a result, are stated in terms of the measuring unit current at the reporting date</span>
          <span>Whether the financial statements are based on a historical cost or a current cost approach</span>
          <span>The identity and level of the price index at the reporting date, and the movement in the index during the current AND the previous reporting period</span>
          <span>The gain or loss on the net monetary position, presented separately</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 10 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>Critical Judgments & Estimates</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cn span2">
        <div class="ctitle cn">Where management judgement matters most</div>
        <div class="two-col">
          <div>
            <div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT JUDGEMENTS</div>
            <div class="dot-list">
              <span>Whether an economy has become (or ceased to be) hyperinflationary, based on the qualitative and quantitative indicators collectively</span>
              <span>Selecting an appropriate general price index where multiple indices are available, or where reliable official data is limited</span>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div>
            <div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT ESTIMATES</div>
            <div class="dot-list">
              <span>Historical dates of acquisition/incurrence used as the basis for restating non-monetary items, where records are incomplete</span>
              <span>Approximating restatement for SOPL items when detailed transaction-date data isn't practically available, often via a suitable average index for the period</span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="callout warn" style="margin-top:8px;margin-bottom:0;"> In economies with genuinely volatile or unreliable official price index data, selecting and defending a "general price index" itself becomes a significant practical and judgemental challenge, sometimes requiring reference to a relatively stable foreign currency's exchange rate movements as a reasonable proxy.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 11 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>IFRS vs US GAAP — Key Differences</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2">
        <div class="ctitle ci">Comparison — IAS 29 vs US GAAP (ASC 830)</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1fr 1fr;"><span>Feature</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-info);">IFRS (IAS 29)</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-warning);">US GAAP</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1fr 1fr;"><span>Fundamental approach</span><span>RESTATE the local-currency financial statements using a general price index, THEN translate if needed</span><span>Use the REPORTING currency of the parent as the functional currency directly for a highly inflationary foreign entity — bypassing separate restatement</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1fr 1fr;"><span>Net monetary position gain/loss</span><span>Explicitly calculated and separately disclosed</span><span>No direct equivalent concept, since restatement itself is not performed the same way</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1fr 1fr;"><span>Hyperinflation threshold</span><span>Qualitative indicators plus ~100% cumulative 3-year guideline</span><span>Broadly similar cumulative 3-year rate guideline used as a practical threshold</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1fr 1fr;"><span>Comparatives</span><span>Restated to the CURRENT period's measuring unit</span><span>Not applicable in the same way, since the parent's stable currency is used directly instead</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="note">This is one of the most philosophically DIFFERENT areas between the two frameworks — IFRS insists on a genuine restatement of the local financial statements themselves, while US GAAP effectively sidesteps the issue by treating the parent's stable currency as the functional currency for the foreign entity, avoiding restatement mechanics altogether.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 12 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s12"><span class="num">12</span><h2>Statement Mapping — Where Every Hyperinflation Adjustment Lands</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs span2">
        <div class="ctitle cs">Comprehensive event-to-statement mapping table</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Event / transaction</span><span>Statement(s) affected</span><span>Notes</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Restatement of non-monetary assets at historical cost</span><span><span class="badge bs">SOFP</span></span><span>Uplifted by the general price index change since acquisition</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Monetary assets/liabilities</span><span>—</span><span>NOT restated; already in current measuring unit</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Restatement of income/expense items</span><span><span class="badge bp">SOPL</span></span><span>Each item restated from its own original transaction date</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Gain/loss on net monetary position</span><span><span class="badge bp">SOPL</span></span><span>Recognised and separately disclosed, unique to hyperinflationary reporting</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Restatement of equity items (share capital, retained earnings)</span><span><span class="badge be">Equity</span></span><span>Treated as non-monetary; restated from the date each component arose</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Restatement of comparative period figures</span><span><span class="badge bs">SOFP</span> <span class="badge bp">SOPL</span></span><span>Restated to the CURRENT period's measuring unit</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Consolidation of a hyperinflationary foreign operation</span><span><span class="badge bs">SOFP</span> <span class="badge bp">SOPL</span></span><span>IAS 29 restatement applied FIRST, then IAS 21 translation</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Cash flow statement</span><span><span class="badge bs">Cash Flow Statement</span></span><span>Also restated in terms of the measuring unit current at the reporting date</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Price index level, movement, and restatement basis</span><span><span class="badge bp">Notes</span></span><span>Mandatory disclosure whenever IAS 29 is applied</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 13 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s13"><span class="num">13</span><h2>Related Standards — Cross-Reference Map</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2">
        <div class="ctitle cd">How IAS 29 connects across the IFRS body of literature</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>Standard</span><span>Nature of the connection</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>IAS 21</span><span>Sequencing rule: an entity whose functional currency is hyperinflationary restates its statements under IAS 29 FIRST, then translates under IAS 21 if a different presentation currency is used</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>IAS 16 / IAS 38</span><span>Supply the historical cost figures for non-monetary PPE and intangible assets that IAS 29 then restates by the general price index</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>IAS 2</span><span>Inventory carrying amounts at historical cost are restated; if inventory is already carried at NRV (a current value), no further index restatement is needed for that item</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>IAS 12</span><span>Deferred tax balances arising from restated non-monetary items must reflect the restated carrying amounts when calculating temporary differences</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>IAS 7</span><span>The statement of cash flows is also restated to the current measuring unit, consistent with the rest of the restated financial statements</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>IFRS 10</span><span>Determines whether a hyperinflationary foreign operation is consolidated, with IAS 29 restatement applied before consolidation adjustments</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 14 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s14"><span class="num">14</span><h2>Quick-Reference Glossary</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="three-col">
      <div class="card cn">
        <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Hyperinflationary economy</div><div class="glossary-def">An economy exhibiting characteristics such as a cumulative 3-year inflation rate approaching or exceeding 100%, along with the qualitative indicators in Section 3.</div></div>
        <div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;"><div class="glossary-term">Measuring unit current at the reporting date</div><div class="glossary-def">The unit of currency value, adjusted for cumulative purchasing power changes, used to express ALL amounts in restated hyperinflationary financial statements.</div></div>
      </div>
      <div class="card cn">
        <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Historical cost approach (under IAS 29)</div><div class="glossary-def">Restating historical cost/depreciated historical cost financial statements by a general price index, rather than switching to a wholly different current-cost accounting system.</div></div>
        <div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;"><div class="glossary-term">Current cost approach (under IAS 29)</div><div class="glossary-def">A less commonly used alternative where items are already stated at current cost, which are then restated to the measuring unit current at the reporting date.</div></div>
      </div>
      <div class="card cn">
        <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Net monetary position</div><div class="glossary-def">The net of an entity's monetary assets and monetary liabilities — its sign (net asset vs net liability) determines whether hyperinflation produces a loss or gain of purchasing power.</div></div>
        <div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;"><div class="glossary-term">Purchasing power</div><div class="glossary-def">The value of currency expressed in terms of the quantity of goods/services it can buy — the concept IAS 29 restatement seeks to preserve consistently across time.</div></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="legend">
      <div class="ltitle">Financial statement key</div>
      <div class="lrow">
        <span class="badge bs">SOFP</span> Statement of financial position (balance sheet)
        <span class="badge bp">SOPL</span> Statement of profit or loss (income statement)
        <span class="badge bo">OCI</span> Other comprehensive income
        <span class="badge be">Equity</span> Statement of changes in equity
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
